Date: Fri, 30 Apr 93 21:38:43 PDT
From: Eli Brandt <ebrandt@jarthur.Claremont.EDU>
Subject: PHIL: GS + Objectivism (+ Extropianism) = ?

> From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
> We need to measure relative entropy, eg "The Mormon religion is
> less entropic than the Episcopalian".

I always had a hard time with thermodynamics -- how are you going to
define the thermodynamic entropy of a religion?  It is not rational
to insist on a scalar measure of everything, or to trust one if you
can construct it.

> I've seen ideas called "entropic" on this list based, not on
> any sort of observation related to organization or physics or information
> theory, but just because the poster disagreed with the idea ideologically.  
> I think that's silly.

It's not a technical term, it's in-group jargon.  Denigrating things
as "entropic" in the technical sense would be pointless.  For
example, you probably have a higher entropy than I, because you are
probably more massive.  On the other hand, I probably have a higher
molar entropy, because I still have a slight fever.  Neither of
these statements tell us much of value.

> > I hereby denounce negentropy as a goal.
> 
> Well then, you have rejected the very basis of Extropianism.

I'll do that too.  
"Extropianism" is a nice name.  It exudes good vibes; it even has a
decent rationale behind it.  But I'm not going to structure my life
around a thermodynamic variable.  Yeah, I know, there's probably
some "principle" that encourages "local reversals of entropy".  I
don't think this is actually an encouragement to go out, buy half a
dozen refrigerators, and run them on the front lawn as a monument to
extropianism.

> measurable sense of destroying human negentropic social relationships
> without building superior replacements.

Extrapolating from chemistry (heh), I would guess that any social
relationship is negentropic.  A given society organized as an
autocratic hierarchy probably has, ceteris paribus, a lower total
entropy than a free society -- the difference will probably be
vanishingly small in relative terms, though.  I think this
illustrates that entropy is a poor guide to policy.  In fact, I
suspect you would agree, and are using "negentropy" in the same sort
of non-technical way as are others.

