Date:     Sat, 1 May 93 9:43:24 GMT
From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject: Critical Generalizations

Sheesh!  I go away for a little over a week, and people flame each other
for criticizing both environmentalism and Objectivism!  It's interesting
to me that while the pro-environmentalists (or anti-anti-enviros) immediately
used the tactic of denying representativeness, the pro-Objectivists
tended more to defend the actual parts of the meme-set.  In other words, we
all admit that there are lots of enviros out there that really do want us
set back to the pleistocene, but some argue that lots more enviros don't
really want that.

Personally, I don't care what the majority of enviros want, because history
isn't determined by what the majority wants, but by what is thought, and
intellectuals are always a minority.  As Margaret Mead said, never doubt
that a small, committed group of individuals can change the world; indeed,
it's the only thing that ever has.

I'll defend to my death that almost every environmentalist and organization
I've ever been aware of is more statist than not, whether it be the Sierra
Club, the Green Party, whatnot.  I can name the exceptions off the top of
my head: the Foundation for Research on Economics and the Environment, and
the Political Economy Research Center.

According to Karl Hess, Jr.'s review of "Green Delusions," the self-styled
moderate environmentalist author criticizes about four or five different
kinds of environmentalism for "extremism," which means too blatant usurpation
or power in my experience.  Free-market enviros are called "fringe."

As for anecdotal experience, I grew up in Berkeley, where 10% of the electorate
registered Green.  I challenge anyone to find a higher registration in the USA
for the Green party.  My grandfather also spend much of his life defending
his forestland and that of his neighbors from the likes of the Sierra Club.
They didn't like the fact that the private organization he founded did a fine
job of management, and wanted to pre-empt his plans with their own designs.
(He started out working for a logging company in the 1920s, clearcut the land,
bought it cheap, and let it grow back, introduced beaver, forget-me-nots,
the beaver built dams which created ponds for brook trout fishing...)

Let's consider an hypothetical non-statist enviro: doesn't buy into any of
the various necessity defenses for statism being propagated such as global
warming (if there was a threat of this, Canadian real estate would be booming),
ozone depletion, overpopulation, a standard of presumed-guilty for anthropogenic
substances by regulatory agencies like the EPA (a standard made double by
their treatment of biogenics as presumed innocent); isn't a Luddite techno-
prohibitionist like Jeremy Rifkin; opposes the Endangered Species Act because
it creates legally fictitious species and, besides, free markets are the best
protection for animals that humans want preserved anyways...

Find me such enviros in an organization with the membership size of the
Sierra Club, and then there'll be some plausibility to the hysterical claim
that most enviros are good guys.  Bullshit.

As for Objectivism, Eric Raymond's got Aristotle all wrong.  His description
applies fairly well to Descartes, but not to Aristotle or the Aristotelian
tradition.  For example, Descartes was a deductivist, exclusively.  Aristotle,
especially in his ethics, held that induction had a big role to play.

And while his essay has a nice narrative flow to it, the facts get in the way
of the story.  The nonsense about noumena and phenomena derive from Kant, not
Aristotle, as well as the vagaries about metaphysics depending upon episte-
mology.

I truly wish that people who made such confident claims about the subject
bothered to learn even the basic terms for what they're talking about.

Rand, following in the Aristotelian tradition, was an epistemological realist,
not a German idealist.  German idealism followed from Kant's "Copernican
revolution" in which he argued that we're incapable of knowing reality because
we have a faculty for this function.  We're blind because we have eyes, deaf
because we have ears.

As far as I can tell, all the various attacks on Randian epistemology stem from
disagreement with her realism.  No more, no less.  It's hardly obvious or
decided tradition (of the level of one of the laws of physics, for example)
that realism is incorrect, yet many here feel free to claim away how it's
silly to talk as if we're at all capable of being aware of what is, anyways.
Korzybski, Robert Anton Wilson, Hume - all these fall along this axis, sort
of.

What they're actually trying to do is account for perceptual relativity, and
the division of knowledge.  All of which are subjects of concern and
interest.  Objectivism has addressed these, and proposed solutions.  Regardless
of the rectitude of them, they exist, and no one of the critics of Rand seems
to have the slightest idea what they are, at least as far as this list goes.
I am on a list for more elevated discussion of Objectivism which includes
some non-Objectivist professional philosophers, and they at least have some
fajki

familiarity with the literature.  The philosophical literature, not just the
fictional novels and a cursory reading of Rand's Intro.

I suppose I'm a bit too vehement in my reaction, but my patience has reached
its limit.  Such nonsense can only be taken so long.  I was sure someone
else would beat me to it and squelch such bad memes, but they haven't.  Too
bad Perry hasn't studied more epistemology and metaphysics, eh?  Then he
could beat me to the punch there as well as so many other places.

