Subject: PHIL:Individualism and Negentropy

Nick Szabo is not a collectivist by Ray Cromwell's definition and
Ray Cromwell is not an individualist by Nick Szabo's definition.
Even Rand didn't accept the straw-man version of individualism
(one may rationally choose to die for one's values). I'm not sure
about Nietzche. I doubt that many 'collectivists' really accept the
values that Rand attributed to them.

In at least one sense, Rand was herself a collectivist. I leave the
explanation of how this could be true as an exercise to the
interested reader. (A hint: did she really accept Ray's definition of
individualism? I'd put a smiley here, but then you'd think I was
joking.)

Similarly, these categories are flaccid enough for someone to claim
that Marx was an individualist.

After reading Hayek this whole debate (historically, not just Ray and
NickUs recapitulation) seems useless. Both sides tended toward non
sequitur. Just because humans are social beings doesn't mean they
need to be slaves of an all powerful state. Nor does a belief in
markets imply that persons must become wealth maximization
calculators.

As for entropy, extropy, and negentropy, I like entropy because it
seems to line up with my other preferences. I see no reason that I
should feel compelled to support it or condemn those who don't.
Similarly, measuring them may bring some insights, but I doubt that
even a sympathetic listener will change their lifestyle just to reduce
their entropy. I want to live longer and better, that's all. Sometimes I
will have to trade one for the other, but I doubt that I will do so on
the basis of entropy.

