Date: Sat, 1 May 93 15:54:43 CDT
From: ddfr@midway.uchicago.edu
Subject: Rational Discrimination and PPL

"At least once a month, they would get stopped by cops, searched,
occasionally pinned to the ground, their papers checked, their drug levels
checked...."

Dean Tribble gives a good example of rational descrimination--police
checking out blacks, who in fact are perfectly respectable students, in a
white area adjacent to a black slum with high crime rates, and asks:

"Does anyone see any solution to this problem?"

In one sense no, in another yes. Under a competitive legal system, the
police may continue to do the same thing, but they (or rather the firm that
employs them) will have to pay, implicitly or explicitly, for the costs
they inflict by doing so. They will therefore have an incentive to try to
minimize those costs. One way is by being very polite to people they stop
who might be in the situation of Dean's friends. Another might be by making
more of an effort not to make the same mistake twice--a data base that
tells them that that particular motorcycle belongs to someone who turned
out to be a Stanford student the last time they stopped him. Rational
discrimination will still exist, and I think should exist, but the costs it
imposes should be reduced substantially.

One approach to reducing such problems is a good system of IDs. One could
imagine a technology where the police can quickly at low cost to all
concerned get enough information about someone to make the mere fact of
race almost irrelevent. But of course, they will still "discriminate"
against anyone who the available evidence suggests is a likely
criminal--for instance, someone living in East Palo Alto who has dropped
out of school because the school is not teaching anything and is working
hard teaching himself skills that he expects to get him out of poverty.

David Friedman
University of Chicago Law School


